          up: {"title":"Spot the envelope errors (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Devon prepared two envelopes. Envelope 1 is to Mr Kelvin Gumbs, Purchasing Manager, Sunrise Supermarket, Cayon Street, Old Road (the inside address reads Mr Kelvin Gumbs), marked CONFIDENTIAL. Envelope 2 is to Mr Ian Wright, Sales Director, Atlantic Marine Supply, 210 Harbor Drive, Miami, Florida 33132, USA, marked URGENT.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that describes an error.","lines":["1. Envelope 1: CONFIDENTIAL typed in capitals in the lower-left corner.","2. Envelope 1: OLD ROAD and ST. KITTS typed on separate lines.","3. Envelope 1: the notation also typed on the letter, after the date and above the inside address.","4. Envelope 2: the ZIP code 33132 typed on its own line below USA.","5. Envelope 2: \"Harbor Drive\" changed to \"Harbour Drive\" to match British spelling.","6. Envelope 2: URGENT typed inside the address block, between the name and the designation.","7. Envelope 2: the return address typed in the top-left corner.","8. Envelope 2: the country typed in lower case as \"usa\".","9. Envelope 1: the name typed as \"Mr K Gumbs\", although the inside address reads \"Mr Kelvin Gumbs\".","10. Both envelopes: the top-right corner left clear for postage."],"correct":[3,4,5,7,8],"win":"Sharp checking. The ZIP code belongs on the town line (MIAMI FL 33132); a proper noun in an address is never respelt; a notation never goes inside the address block; the country goes in capitals on the last line; and the envelope must match the inside address exactly.","miss":"Check every line for position (corners and lines), accuracy against the inside address, postal order (postcode with the town, country last in capitals) and whether a proper noun has been changed."}
